  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- ${e}^{x}{y}^{\u2033}+y={x}^{2}$\n- $y{y}^{\u2033}+{y}^{\u2032}=x$\n- $3{y}^{\u2033}-{x}^{2}{y}^{\u2032}+4{y}^{2}=x$",
  "worked_solution": "\u0410 second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{\u2033}+b(x){y}^{\u2032}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}{y}^{\u2033}+y={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)={e}^{x},b(x)=0,c(x)=1,$ and $f(x)={x}^{2}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\ny{y}^{\u2033}+{y}^{\u2032}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $y{y}^{\u2033}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n3{y}^{\u2033}-{x}^{2}{y}^{\u2032}+4{y}^{2}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $4{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
